      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Background",
          "content": "Half of Americans have at least 1 chronic disease. Many in this group, particularly racial/ethnic minorities, lacked insurance coverage and access to care before the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (ACA) was enacted."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Objective",
          "content": "To determine whether the ACA has had an effect on insurance coverage, access to care, and racial/ethnic disparities among adults with chronic disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Design",
          "content": "Quasi-experimental policy intervention."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Setting",
          "content": "Nationally representative, noninstitutionalized sample in the United States."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Patients",
          "content": "606 277 adults aged 18 to 64 years with a chronic disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Intervention",
          "content": "Implementation of ACA provisions on 1 January 2014."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Measurements",
          "content": "Self-reported insurance coverage, having a checkup, having a personal physician, and not having to forgo a needed physician visit because of cost."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Results",
          "content": "After the ACA was implemented, insurance coverage increased by 4.9 percentage points (95% CI, 4.4 to 5.4), not having to forgo a physician visit increased by 2.4 percentage points (CI, 1.9 to 2.9), and having a checkup increased by 2.7 percentage points (CI, 2.2 to 3.4). Having a personal physician did not change (0.3 percentage points [CI, -0.2 to 0.8]). All outcomes varied considerably by state, and coverage increased more in states that expanded Medicaid. Although racial/ethnic minorities had greater improvements in some outcomes, approximately 1 in 5 black and 1 in 3 Hispanic persons with a chronic disease continued to lack coverage and access to care after ACA implementation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Limitation",
          "content": "The study examined data from only the first year of the ACA's major coverage expansion provisions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Conclusion",
          "content": "Although the ACA increased coverage and access for persons with chronic disease, substantial gaps remain, particularly for minorities and those in Medicaid nonexpansion states."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Primary Funding Source",
          "content": "None."
        }
      ]},
